WEBVTT

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:11.970
 My religion is called Pikiyoush. Pikiyoush in translation to English is "World Renewal" and "Fix the World".

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:25.890
 So the Karuk people are the "Fix the World" people. Pikiyoush tells us how to treat each other, manage the resources, harvest the resources, distribution of the resources, all under the ceremonial umbrella.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:31.400
 And those are the things that I am trying to incorporate into people's lives.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:41.950
 A treaty was made with a cut of people to give up 1.4 million square acres of land for a reservation that would be able to fish, hunt, and gather forever.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:48.480
 Because there was gold in our country, the Congress at that time disallowed that treaty to be put into law.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.070
 So we have the dubious distinction of being one of 18 tribes in California with an unratified treaty.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:03.670
 So every time we turn around, there's jurisdictional boundary issues that has led to us to manage on the cutting edge.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:16.010
 In this process, we are teaching academics how we manage the world in a totally different way of our European neighbors' colonizers.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:21.970
 Traditional knowledge is nothing but local knowledge. The spirit of the landscape connected to human beings.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.570
 So I am an expert at fishing, which that's the only thing I'll claim.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.570
 Everything else, I'm an expert at connecting people.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:35.140
 And I have this vision, a traditional knowledge vision, that has been supported by a great amount of people.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.140
 And I wouldn't be here today without those people.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:46.480
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.480
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:53.680
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.970
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:07.650
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.250
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:16.850
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:24.130
 Ron Reed is a traditional dipnet fisherman and cultural biologist for the Karuk tribe of California.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:35.130
 He develops plans for eco-cultural revitalization, leads youth cultural education camps, and fosters collaborative research at the nexus of traditional ecological knowledge and Western science.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:46.130
 Ron plays a critical role in increasing public awareness about the impacts of colonization on the spiritual and physical health of his people and on the ecological integrity of the Karuk ancestral lands.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:56.130
 He is the founder of the Karuk-UC Berkeley collaborative and works with nearby tribes, UC Berkeley, and the USDA on the Klamath Basin Tribal Food Security Project.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:10.000
 So I'm here with Ron Reed, and I think I want to start, I think what's important to kind of set the scene to talk about is, can you describe where you grew up and where you live now?

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.480
 Yeah, again, thank you very much for having me today.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.870
 Well, first of all, my name is Ron Reed.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:23.930
 I'm employed as a cultural biologist for the Karuk tribe of California, up in extreme northern California.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:32.180
 Extreme northern California means, it doesn't mean Santa Cruz, it means northern California to me, up under the Oregon border.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:40.640
 Our ancestral territory spreads into the Oregon, as well as California, so the jurisdictional issues and every road we take.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.350
 But like I said, we're up under the Oregon border in the Klamath River Basin.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:54.730
 And that's basically, for you people who don't really know, it's somewhere in between Wairica, California, and Eureka, California.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.690
 Our ancestral territory spreads through five counties.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.140
 We have two forests inside of our ancestral territory.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:14.480
 So every time we turn around, there's jurisdictional boundary issues that has led to us to manage on the cutting edge.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:32.150
 We have memorandums of agreement, MOAs with the Forest Service, with Caltrans, with a whole host of federal and state agencies that have to consult with the Karuk tribe, being a federally recognized tribe.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:37.440
 So we have consultation issues that we work on.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.860
 And a lot of the work I do has to do with food and culture.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:51.920
 And so I'm a very humble and proud man of being able to be sitting here in front of you talking about those things.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:59.010
 Ron, I think you mentioned last night that the unratified treaty between the Karuk tribe and the U.S. government underlies.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:09.230
 A lot of us need to negotiate all these different kinds of agreements with the Forest Service and sovereignty over the traditional land or part of the traditional land.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.230
 Can you talk about that a little bit?

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.520
 Sure.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.520
 I was born into this world looking at the glass half empty.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:33.680
 Fortunately for myself, I was born into a family, a medicinal family, a very traditional family of the Karuk tribe, where we had medicine people, we had medicine doctors, we had hunters, fishermen, and gatherers.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.810
 And so we respect the landscape that is connected to our religion.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.850
 My religion is called Piquiauj.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:51.200
 Piquiauj in translation to English is world renewal and fix the world.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.390
 So the Karuk people are the fix the world people.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.800
 And we've shared that religion with other tribes in the basin.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.210
 The Hoopa and the Yurok and the Klamath tribes up above.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:09.420
 So with that relationship, we all work together in continuity as one in the Klamath River Basin until contact.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:18.080
 Contact has a whole variety of issues, socially, physically, mentally, all those different things.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:24.800
 And I've been able to evolve from being a product of intergenerational trauma.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.050
 Mining occurred up in the Klamath River Basin.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:40.620
 A treaty was made with the Karuk people to give up 1.4 million square acres of land for a reservation that we'd be able to fish, hunt, and gather forever.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:48.920
 Because there was gold in our country, the Congress at that time disallowed that treaty to become put into law.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:57.900
 So we have the dubious distinction of being one of 18 tribes in California with an unratified treaty.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:03.440
 They didn't give us back our land, they didn't give us back anything, except for a shattered existence.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.350
 That shattered existence is what I grew up knowing to be the Karuk world.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:13.600
 Growing up, I wasn't very proud to be a Karuk.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:21.250
 Even though my people told me how important our culture is to the world,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:28.650
 to understand that my family participated in the world renewal ceremonies at the highest level.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:39.060
 And to be told that the blood that runs through your veins is to create a voice that would be and could be heard around the world.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.890
 Growing up, I couldn't understand that, but I was taught that anyway.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:08:01.040
 I wasn't taught my culture because epiphany of my great grandmother, whom was an Indian doctor, told my mother, whom she taught all the old ways, that she needed to teach us the new way so we can survive.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:08.820
 Very confusing for me as a child, as a young child, I was taught a lot of the social values, the things I talk about and express today.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:18.650
 But at some point, probably around seventh, eighth grade, society, peer pressure, whatever it is, I didn't quite figure it out yet.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:27.530
 But I feel like at that point in my life is when I started noticing that I was poor and I didn't have the things that other people had.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.530
 And it felt like there's a social disruption in my world.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.350
 And I became very angry and I wanted to.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:43.620
 And my lifelong dream at that point was to move off the river, play basketball and never come back.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:54.930
 But I was also taught that if you have this medicine that runs through your veins and if you are put here on earth to do a certain thing, that you could never acquire happiness or anything else except for that certain thing.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:59.080
 It took me a great number of years to figure that out.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:06.840
 And if it wasn't for those early teachings of traditional knowledge, of self-respect, self-dignity, I wouldn't be here today.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:16.420
 I floundered in the wind for a lot of years trying to figure these things out following my gut feeling, which was a broken heart and anger.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.570
 I played basketball until I couldn't anymore.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.630
 Then I realized that I am brown.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:24.630
 I am from the Clameth River.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.820
 I do have medicine in my veins.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:27.820
 And I had a family.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:28.770
 I just got a family.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:39.240
 And until I got my family, until I married my wife, Robin, and my beautiful six children, five boys and one girl, I was very selfish with me.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.880
 I didn't respect myself enough to do the things that were necessary to be a responsible adult.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:54.400
 But once I started having my children, all the teachings of my people started ringing very true to my ears.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:03.020
 At that point in time, I had to start cleaning up my act and start realizing that I was put here for a reason.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:18.050
 And the things I was doing is going to be something that allows my family to reach into the future with the same level of happiness that a few generations before me.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:23.050
 A lot of atrocities happened to our people, a lot of baggage, a lot of intergenerational trauma.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:32.020
 And the more I understand those processes, the more available I am to the community.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:43.900
 I have taken on a lot of those atrocities in my own self, relearning my culture, relearning my ways, and a lot of times by non-Natives, which is very embarrassing.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:51.300
 Very tough to work in those confines, which I relate to a lot of people up in my country today.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:59.460
 I'm not fading away from our culture because we don't have the proper teachings in place because of the stripped identity that we no longer have.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:06.940
 So, with my teachings from my family, I was able to meet some very strong people in the academic world.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:17.340
 Dr. Kari Norgard, years back in 2005, we did a denied access to traditional foods in the Klamath River hydroelectric relicensing process.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:30.250
 Before this point, no group of people of race or color has ever accused the federal government of destroying their health or their social well-being with a federal project.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:37.870
 What we determined in that document was that the Kuduk people were way below the poverty line.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:49.340
 Three, four times the national average in basic and other in health consequences such as diabetes, hypertension, mental illnesses, and all those different things.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.340
 And it brought a great awareness to the community around us.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.720
 It's not just a fish running up the river.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.970
 It's a world ideology.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:01.750
 I'm not fighting for the fish.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:07.350
 I'm not fighting for the landscape for any other reason that that's my relation as part of my religion.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:16.030
 If there's something very close to me that doesn't have a voice, it's up to me to speak on behalf of that creature or that living thing or that entity.

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:22.600
 I'm an introvert turned extrovert.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:30.880
 And I like solitude, but I also like situations like this where it makes me feel very good in my heart.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:39.600
 My spirit rises when I'm able to feel like I'm contributing to the success of people, communities other than the Kuduk people.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:52.710
 It's ironic because I need this other community to shed light onto these issues at a greater level to determine what is the success process that we're searching for so diligently.

00:12:52.710 --> 00:13:00.050
 People from all walks of life are here at this conference, and yet we have the same basic needs.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:01.560
 We need to be a part of something.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.560
 We need good food.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:11.550
 We need to be able to contribute to the next generation and not hold our next generation hostage for the bad mistakes we're making today for tomorrow.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:13.550
 So we're here to do all those things.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:27.180
 And fortunately, for me, in my perspective, all these things fit under the ceremonial umbrella of the Kaduk religion, Pikyawish.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:40.070
 Pikyawish tells us how to treat each other, manage the resources, harvest the resources, distribution of the resources, all under the ceremonial umbrella.

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:48.730
 And those are the things that I am incorporating into, trying to incorporate into people's lives.

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:58.650
 I've been able to raise awareness to the federal, the state governments, key stakeholders, and some tribal members.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:01.900
 And that was a pretty hard fight.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:06.900
 And it took me a few years to do that, or we, a lot of us, you know, participate in that process.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:22.520
 But once we got to where we need to be as far as bringing focus to our tribal community, the last thing I thought I would be troubled with is getting our tribal people to the table.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:29.840
 It's exhausting to get tribal people to the table for a lot of different reasons.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:35.710
 And our contemporary society thinks that we have these blanket fixes.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:46.230
 Social programs come in and say, okay, teenage alcoholics, teenage pregnancies, they put all these different categories.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:51.320
 But from a tribal perspective, you learn all those categories at a very young age.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:15:01.370
 Before you ever have awareness of who you want to be or what your interests are as a brain growing and living in this world and what you want to become,

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:07.370
 you already have this self-respect, integrity, and dignity of being a kind of person.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:09.340
 This is what that stands for.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:15.330
 So that should allow you to deal with any of the pitfalls that life has to offer.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:18.760
 And believe me, I got out of a couple of pitfalls.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:25.760
 So I rely solely on the traditional knowledge, my awareness of the environment, and the awareness of the community.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:31.880
 Something that I wasn't so gifted with when I was, you know, in the society I lived in.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:36.970
 Through this awareness process, I've created great friends with networking.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:41.160
 The work I do is basically doesn't really have a lot of funding.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:56.780
 And so I've been bouncing around from fisheries to environmental justice to traditional knowledge to food systems to, you know, whatever this process, however it's perceived, we get funding.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:06.510
 Because we're a federally recognized tribe without a reservation, we do not have resource money for our natural resources.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.510
 So we're a grant-seeking tribe.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:16.390
 So it really harnesses our ability to create a strategy because, of course, we all know with grants you have these deliverables.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:22.410
 And a lot of times the deliverables don't pertain to tribal government or tribal vision.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:28.500
 So we're often having to have several grants into one process to make it happen.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.550
 And it's really, it gets really kind of messy.

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:35.190
 But there's such an awareness now because of climate change.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:41.250
 Climate change has come into our purview and it's amazing.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:45.680
 It's amazing on both sides of the aisle.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:56.650
 It's amazing the impacts that climate change has, but it's amazing that the ability of the Kuduk people, of indigenous people around the world to address the issues that only we can address.

00:16:56.650 --> 00:17:01.190
 The only proven process on this planet is indigenous space.

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:03.380
 We're all indigenous at some point.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:19.840
 Socialization has really disrupted the spiritual flow of human beings to the earth, back to the earth as a form of food, creating energy back to the people and so forth and so on.

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:26.440
 That's what I'm trying to reestablish in my mind so I can reestablish that in other folks' minds.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:38.320
 And unfortunately, because it's intergenerational trauma, I have to seek a lot of this information outside of my tribal comfort zone.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:48.360
 And in doing so, I get state-of-the-art type communication with people that I plug into my perspective, which makes it even more powerful.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.510
 So that's the beauty of working within a community that isn't all tribal.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:57.510
 Because we need help to do what we do because we don't have the resources available to us.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:00.810
 We are a true tribal environmental justice tribe.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.860
 We're seeking to do all these great things, but we don't have the resources.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:25.330
 We have all these ideas that we share consistently with the federal state governments that often take those issues out of context and put them out on the map or on the playing field or in the history as far as ink on paper and without any type of intellectual sovereignty given back to the Kuduk people.

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:28.970
 So one of the biggest things in my world is intellectual sovereignty.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.540
 The Forest Service come into this world.

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:33.540
 I use the Forest Service.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:34.540
 That's our biggest adversary.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:36.540
 Ninety-five percent of ancestral territories owned by the federal government.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:37.540
 The Forest Service owns that.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:41.540
 So we've been having a battering ram type of relationship with the Forest Service.

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:43.540
 So it's kind of a blessing.

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.540
 In some ways, we do not have a reservation.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:50.920
 We don't have all the social issues that reservations have, but we don't have the resources available to us.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:56.240
 So we don't have a specific place we can call home, but we have ancestral territory.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:03.460
 So that gives us, because of the government to government consultation, ancestral territory is our management area.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:10.800
 So we have 1.4 million acres that the federal government and the state governments have to consult with us when they want to do things.

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:11.800
 So that's a blessing.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:17.050
 So I've been able to participate in a lot of those issues, bringing traditional knowledge into the forefront.

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:26.070
 In doing so, I got appointed to the USDA FRAC, the Forest Research Advisory Committee back in D.C.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:30.070
 The first time a traditional knowledge component was added to that council.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:40.090
 And the second year into that process, we, the council, hired a social scientist, a doctorate, Kennelly Kim.

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.410
 I remember her name.

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:43.410
 Not a very good name.

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.410
 I remember her because she's so important to this movement.

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:56.010
 And we're trying to figure out what is the best way to deal with climate change, wildfire suppression, and traditional knowledge.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.230
 Bam!

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:59.230
 That's just kind of like what we live for.

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:00.320
 Yeah.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:07.210
 So we're creating a process of using traditional knowledge for prescribed burns.

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:25.530
 And we have recently, well, not recently, in the last five years, we, with the help of UC Berkeley, I'm a co-founder of, along with Tom Carlson and Jennifer Sauerwein, I'm a co-founder of the Kaduk Tribe UC Berkeley Collaborative.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:35.070
 UC Berkeley come up and help me quantify our traditional ecological knowledge perspective.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:38.410
 Prove it to people.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.410
 In that process, we're able to prove these things.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:46.600
 And now climate change has happened where they're asking, you know, they're asking for critical answers.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:54.700
 Back in the fracking D.C., one of the biggest topics back there is budget borrowing for wildland fire suppression.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:21:02.230
 And Congress has made a rule, we'll give you more money right now, you guys have to get a handle on this because you can't borrow anymore.

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:11.630
 So you have to find a way to create a process, an economical, viable, sustainable process with wildland fire.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:17.580
 And so, hence my availability to this frat council.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.020
 Yeah.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.070
 Pretty honored.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:22.070
 Pretty humbled.

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:27.820
 Because my constituents back there are pretty high academically profiled.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:31.010
 And I would say I'm not.

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:38.320
 And so I'm there, the single purpose of traditional knowledge, but we are, but my traditional knowledge encompasses everybody around the table.

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:49.370
 So it's very exciting to share my ideas and to open, to open minded folks, because they realize that we got to get a handle on this budget borrowing issue.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:22:02.920
 And there's nothing else on the landscape right now, pardon the pun, that allows us to fight catastrophic wildland fire with any kind of beneficial results.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:13.460
 If you're representing traditional knowledge and you're working with a bunch of scientists, I've always felt like there's the boundaries between one kind of knowledge and the other are a little bit artificial.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:22.410
 But yet there's so much in terms of what's, what's recognized and what's considered more legitimate that it just gets tricky to navigate.

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:35.910
 What's for you when you're, when you're having those conversations, when you're going back and forth with scientists and policymakers, what, what are the points of tension and what are the, what are the points of creativity and that kind of a collaboration?

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:49.350
 I guess one of the biggest points of contention for me is when I first started this traditional knowledge, being part of this movement was people understanding what I'm saying.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:52.570
 In the beginning, it was like, it's like a foreign language.

00:22:52.570 --> 00:22:53.570
 People don't get it.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:23:09.180
 For instance, I'm talking now of prescribed fire managers that have worked in the landscape for 20 years before we come up to this food security grant from the USDA and prescribed fire.

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:13.380
 Fire, we've been prescribing fire to the landscape for thousands of years.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:24.670
 And the park-like existence of the natural resources when, when the Europeans first landed on this planet was because of our nurturing, our understanding, our spiritual connection with the landscape.

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:32.040
 And so, therefore, they stripped us of that connection, but now we're revitalizing that.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:33.950
 Excuse me, what is your question? I'm sorry.

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:38.040
 In those conversations with scientists, where, where's that contention?

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:48.260
 Okay, so, which leads me, I guess I was on the right track, which leads me to language, which leads me to ability to learn.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:54.450
 And so, I had to do my due diligence in a very non-academic way of going to meetings.

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:57.450
 I felt being thrown to the wolves.

00:23:57.450 --> 00:24:05.500
 And hydroelectric relicensing was the first time I was a tribal lead on such a highly profiled process.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:13.190
 And to get my points across was very contentious in the beginning because I couldn't articulate the way I'm kind of trying to articulate now.

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:20.190
 Now, I didn't have the ability, but my mindset wasn't big enough at that point to understand what it takes to create movement.

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:24.220
 And so, I had to do my due diligence and get the right language.

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:39.300
 Unfortunately, in a non-academic way, I was going to these meetings and talking these issues and getting the snot knocked out of me by lawyers, by stakeholders, by federal officials of not understanding what I'm saying.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:45.300
 And their line of rhetoric and management was continual and very strong.

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:51.330
 And that's where the world is all leaning towards those type of prescriptions.

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:57.580
 And so, the more I got beat up, the more other sympathetic people around the table were throwing.

00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:59.830
 I think what he's trying to say is this.

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:02.750
 Once I get that, I put that in my arsenal or my quiver.

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:07.860
 And pretty soon I have, so right now I have a lot of arrows in my quiver because of that.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:16.590
 Being, you know, learning the language, learning how to communicate, and learning what is it that other people can understand.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.900
 Unfortunately, those things are economics and health.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:26.900
 Some of the things that we've done some really great work on, and so it grabs people's attention.

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:35.900
 So, we did the denied access to traditional foods, which then led us to this grant that we're on right now with this Coduc Tribe UC Berkeley Collaborative.

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:42.340
 Dr. Jennifer Sourwine wrote a grant, a highly competitive grant to the USDA.

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:46.400
 There's a NEFA AFRI Klamath Basin Food Security Grant.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:55.400
 And the grant is for enhancing tribal health and food security in the Klamath Basin of Oregon and California by building a sustainable regional food system.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:59.060
 Five years, multiple objectives.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:06.060
 So, we're out doing K-12 curriculum development, building a traditional knowledge Western science compatibility chart.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:15.060
 So, basically, when we're in the sciences, we can bring in traditional knowledge experts to come in and tell us what science really is and how it evolved to this point.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:30.380
 In this process, we are teaching academics of how we manage the world in a totally different way of our European neighbors' colonizers.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:38.420
 So, in that process, we're able to distinguish between cultural pride and science.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:43.450
 And so, that's kind of like what I do is try to connect people in these areas.

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:49.450
 I'm not a, like I said, intergenerational trauma doesn't make me a cultural expert.

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:53.440
 I'm a traditional dip net fisherman at Ishtapishy Falls.

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:57.470
 You can go on the internet and Google Krook Tribe, Klamath River Fishery.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:00.470
 My name will come up and show me dipping down at Ishtapishy Falls.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:03.720
 So, I'm an expert at fishing, which that's the only thing I'll claim.

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.720
 Everything else, I'm an expert at connecting people.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:14.260
 And I have this vision, a traditional knowledge vision, that has been supported by a great amount of people.

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:16.190
 And I wouldn't be here today without those people.

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:21.820
 But the spirit of the people who walk before me is the reason why I'm here.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:26.430
 But what made me find that trail was the people who walk after me.

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:29.430
 The people who live with me right today are my children.

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:37.300
 I owe them a better way of life because of my heritage responsibility, because of my belief in my religion.

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:41.430
 That is something I'm destined to do.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:48.150
 Growing up in poverty, I had some big issues with my mother, not understanding what I do now.

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:54.360
 I was able to connect with her before she passed away, which leads me to this work that I do today.

00:27:54.360 --> 00:28:12.830
 The spirit of her and her people who walk before her is the spirit that comes from me.

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:17.250
 That's the spirit I project.

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:20.200
 It's a great responsibility, but it's an honor responsibility.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:25.600
 And sometimes I feel like it's a burden.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.300
 More importantly, it's a responsibility of mine.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:42.810
 You talked a little bit last night, or one of my favorite parts of what you were saying is the sense of anger growing up, and then how you still feel it.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:47.860
 And it seems like that's part of this responsibility you're talking about.

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:48.860
 Is that right?

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:51.280
 Is that a transformation?

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:53.280
 How does that transformation work?

00:28:53.280 --> 00:29:02.340
 Well, very stubborn, very angry, very set in my ways, very strong minded.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:07.060
 I was strong minded to be bad.

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:09.060
 I was strong minded to be antisocial.

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:13.180
 That's how I was expressing myself to how I viewed the world.

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:16.690
 And I was getting into more trouble, more trouble, you know.

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:20.690
 And, you know, alcoholism fell into that, you know.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:27.500
 And, you know, drug addiction was a part of that.

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:38.490
 But when it was time to take care of my family and my babies, it was time to figure out who I was as a person.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:45.070
 And I had this foundation of knowledge that was unexpressed at that point.

00:29:45.070 --> 00:30:01.650
 And now I'm trying to use that inherent responsibility to project not only to my tribal community but to the rest of the world so we can figure out what is the best common ground approach that we can use to get people on board with traditional knowledge.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.650
 Traditional knowledge is nothing but local knowledge.

00:30:03.650 --> 00:30:07.650
 The spirit of the landscape connected to human beings.

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:13.860
 It's amazing that the USDA actually put a social scientist on this committee.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.860
 Probably just as amazing as traditional knowledge on this community.

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:26.040
 So now we have an ability to articulate our issues and concerns and to develop these issues and concerns at the highest level.

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:31.040
 The great work we're doing on the Klamath River right now is with prescribed burning.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:38.070
 And that's basically all the federal state agencies and key stakeholders around the table doing consensus driven processes.

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:40.070
 That's a common ground approach.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:41.070
 Zones of agreement.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:42.070
 What are the things we agree on?

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:45.070
 We've been fighting since contact and what we disagree about.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:50.070
 This may be the first time in the history of the United States that we're working on things that we can all agree on.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:58.220
 You mentioned that climate change opened up this possibility for a different way of looking at things or a different way of collaborating.

00:30:58.220 --> 00:31:07.560
 Yeah, I alluded to that earlier when I said basically that climate, you know, that there's nothing else on the landscape that allows us to understand climate change.

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:17.300
 And there's nothing else that can create a level of natural resource management that has positive impacts to climate change.

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:26.810
 Our forest up there in the Klamath River Basin eats up a lot of this carbon and produces oxygen.

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:38.410
 So the biggest thing is not only the economic devastation it does to our federal budget, but it also bankrupts our carbon emissions that go out into space from these catastrophic wildfires.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:45.690
 So there's a so there's some big issues out there that these federal government, they have to deal with.

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.690
 And it's not like it's an epiphany that they've received.

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:53.690
 I think we've been telling them all along how we need to manage the landscape, how we need to manage the people, et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.690
 But there's no reason to hear us.

00:31:55.690 --> 00:32:05.140
 Now, climate change has given us the opportunity to voice our opinions loud and clear about what our perspective of the world is.

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:17.200
 And with the quantification process with Berkeley and other academic institution nowadays with this movement, they're able to use Western science to do what it does best to quantify our process.

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:19.200
 We shouldn't have to go out and prove what we're doing.

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:22.200
 It's a process that we've had for thousands of years.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.200
 So we got it backwards a little bit.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:28.200
 We shouldn't have to go prove something with a pilot project before we get more funding to do it.

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:32.200
 We should just kind of like they should come up and just quantify our effort and realize what we're doing.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:48.380
 So that's what really Berkeley in this relationship with Berkeley is doing is coming and quantifying and putting it into Western science understanding to see how does traditional knowledge benefit climate change and all the social aspects of our community around us.

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:53.380
 So those are the things that I'm expressing not only today, every meeting I go to.

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:59.570
 And again, my biggest impact needs to be with my tribal community.

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:07.490
 And so that's the part that I'm really struggling with now at this point is we got the picture on the table about natural resource management.

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:11.590
 How do we come back and manage our people socially with the environment we have today?

00:33:11.590 --> 00:33:14.590
 And so some of the things I've been talking about are all the different pieces.

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:19.590
 This new grant we have, we're in the fifth year of our grant.

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:32.570
 And so we are really looking forward to putting a lot of our visionary processes to play and accumulate a stepping stone from this food security grant into a climate change grant.

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:38.640
 Unfortunately, that's the way we work and I'm not sure how to deal with that type of issue.

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:57.040
 But we just and I'm just relying on the larger community of professionals and academia to figure out how can they help me build this climate change traditional knowledge vision that encompasses the world rather than just the biased community that you come from.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:34:00.330
 And I think that brings people together.

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:05.330
 It's a spirit of Mother Earth connecting with the great creator, coming back through people.

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:11.540
 The energy comes back from the people through food and redistributes through the landscape once again.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:15.540
 So those are the things that we're working on and we're pretty proud of it.

00:34:15.540 --> 00:34:19.630
 And I'm doing the best I can.

00:34:19.630 --> 00:34:26.630
 I have three children in college right now, you know, and they're all at home working in natural resource management.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:33.540
 So I'm honored and humbled at the same time to be able to do the work I'm doing.

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:39.210
 And I've been meeting a wonderful amount of people along the way.

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:46.360
 And this movement really seems to be growing some really strong legs because we've been walking.

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:47.360
 First, we was crawling.

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:48.360
 Now we're walking.

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:51.380
 I can feel a little gallop starting to happen.

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:53.410
 Has food always been a part of it for you?

00:34:53.410 --> 00:34:57.630
 Has fishing and wild foods always been a part of it?

00:34:57.630 --> 00:34:58.630
 Oh, yeah.

00:34:58.630 --> 00:34:59.630
 My ceremonies.

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:02.030
 Yeah.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:10.030
 My first memories of my life were down there dancing the white deerskin dance with my grandpa and my close relations because there wasn't a lot of people participating.

00:35:10.030 --> 00:35:26.680
 And in the same timeframe, I remember going out with my uncles and I remember this rainy day when my first memories, my rainy day up on a family gathering area where we was picking acorns up and tan oak mushrooms.

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:30.150
 And my uncles come by.

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:31.150
 Okay, everybody get down.

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:32.990
 They went to there was in the service.

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:34.340
 The military.

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:37.340
 And so they said, okay, everybody down, everybody down.

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:39.340
 So we all kind of like got down to the ground and you hear a pop.

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:45.020
 Pretty soon, here they come with a big buck.

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:48.620
 That was my first experience, you know.

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:57.710
 So it's the ceremonies and natural resource management or, yeah, harvest still sticks in my mind.

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:02.050
 And I think that allows me to be here today.

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:08.050
 I was taught the things, the integrity of who we are as a people, the blood that ran through my veins, the atrocities that happened to our people,

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:13.370
 that we have to create a change even if it is out of our comfort zone.

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:37.310
 Having to live in a contemporary society without the cultural knowledge that was passed down from generation to generation to generation that stopped at my generation was one of the toughest things I ever had to go through.

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:45.470
 Teaching a lot of these things or learning a lot of these things from non-natives is a humbling experience.

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:56.740
 But it's an experience I respect because I can go teach my tribal community these things now.

00:36:56.740 --> 00:37:04.890
 And I can relieve them of the embarrassment, the anxiety of, first of all, going to the event.

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:09.700
 Secondly, learning from a non-native.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:14.580
 I have a strong shoulders, I have big shoulders, strong shoulders.

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:18.480
 And I was put here on this earth to do these things.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:19.480
 I truly believe that.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:25.840
 I'll go to the ends of this earth to do what I believe in, which I have.

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:31.840
 In the hydraulic licensing, we went to Scotland and demonstrated over there to the richest stakeholders in the world, Scottish power.

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:33.760
 And we rolled them.

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:36.760
 Unfortunately, they sold the dams to Warren Buffett.

00:37:36.760 --> 00:37:40.580
 Warren Buffett now owns the dams and we're working on him and we're getting the dams out.

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.580
 We've already worked him over too.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:45.580
 So anyway, now we're going to get the dams out in 2020.

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:47.580
 So that's just a stepping stone.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:56.570
 And all the things that I've done in that process allows me to be here today with help of academia, with help of good friends and the help of a strong family.

00:37:56.570 --> 00:38:02.740
 My wife is probably one of the strongest unheard of person in this movement.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.890
 And unfortunately, she was here today.

00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:07.890
 And now they've been corralling her.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:13.720
 And she not only raised my children, but she's helped me be who I am.

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:14.720
 And it's a beautiful relationship.

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:22.110
 And I'm pretty honored to be able to work on these issues at such a level that I am.

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.300
 And yeah.

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.870
 You're not the only one we've talked to for this podcast, Chelsea and I.

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:34.160
 I mean, few people on this that we've interviewed.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:44.380
 A black woman, a couple of women from India have said like this feeling that something that this huge thing has been lost.

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:50.550
 Our ways of what we know about food, what we know about ourselves and our health have been lost.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:52.550
 It's just the weight of that.

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:54.510
 It's just almost it's too much.

00:38:54.510 --> 00:39:00.410
 Too much to describe in words what's been lost through colonialism.

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:14.820
 And so rebuilding that rebuilding that seems complicated in a in a in a contemporary world.

00:39:14.820 --> 00:39:27.110
 It's I guess what does it take to rebuild a knowledge in even given the reality that this unhelpful amount of knowledge has been lost?

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:34.760
 Easy, one word, sacred, two words, sacred fire.

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:46.650
 Over 90% of our plants, our traditional plants, our cultural plants need fire to reproduce.

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:54.830
 So our whole landscape was based on using fire as a primary force management tool.

00:39:54.830 --> 00:40:01.570
 You learn so much by watching nature by observation.

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:05.570
 You throw a fire through someplace and you only see a certain amount of plants.

00:40:05.570 --> 00:40:09.720
 You come back the year after a fire, two years after a fire.

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:13.730
 The diversity of that landscape has changed dramatically.

00:40:13.730 --> 00:40:16.390
 Traditional plants are available now.

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:20.390
 And now it kind of provokes me to think, OK, what were these plants used for?

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:21.350
 We're in.

00:40:21.350 --> 00:40:31.120
 OK, then you start doing research through other ethnographic information through different folks that tribal folks have put together.

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:43.650
 And understanding that we have a lot of the answers to society's problems within our own traditional management movement.

00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:59.490
 So I've been doing my best to try to understand that, you know, our traditional knowledge, interviewing elders and working on these natural resource issues and learning incrementally what it is to be cut out.

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:18.560
 And so now what it's allowed me to do now is to articulate those issues alongside of academics, alongside of federal, state policymakers, and to have an impact, a potential change the way they view the world.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:21.910
 And that in itself is tremendous.

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:30.510
 And without the academic community, without people like you, without people like people at this conference, this doesn't happen.

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:39.960
 I mean, people at this conference are so polished that everything I say, I just see a hundred heads after shaking their head, yeah, yeah.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.960
 And don't do that to me because I just poured on after that.

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:53.950
 So, you know, so, I mean, it just really from, it's really been a great step from the first day I started talking about traditional knowledge, using fire in the landscape to protect our fish.

00:41:53.950 --> 00:41:57.940
 You should have seen the biologists look in this with a state of confusion at that point.

00:41:57.940 --> 00:41:59.940
 And that's been 15 years ago.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:03.940
 So now we've been doing a lot of work since then and people know it now.

00:42:03.940 --> 00:42:05.940
 So now we're going up the landscape and fixing the landscape.

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:07.940
 And now they're saying, well, what about the fish?

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:08.940
 So it's kind of coming around.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:10.940
 A lot of the different things are coming around full circle.

00:42:10.940 --> 00:42:23.160
 And I think it's just a tribute to maybe my lack of traditional knowledge that I have this inherent responsibility to provide a foundation of traditional well-being to my family in the future.

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:33.290
 So those are the things that I'm striving to create, to have a template for my own people to follow if they want to reach this level of harmony.

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:38.700
 Something that our tribal ceremonies are, that's it.

00:42:38.700 --> 00:42:42.820
 Diversity and harmony are the two primary factors of why we do these things.

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:50.420
 And the respect that it's a true interdisciplinary team to try managing.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:43:04.450
 And so those are the things that I incorporate in my work, my world ideology that I'm still learning, and trying to find a better way for not only my tribal community, but the world.

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:07.320
 Pick you out.

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:09.450
 Thanks so much, Ron.

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:14.530
 I think that's been a great interview.

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:22.130
 I just wanted to ask you if there's anything I haven't asked about or if there's something else you'd like to tell to this audience.

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:36.020
 Well, you know, right now I think you've exhausted me, but I just feel like, you know, that this is real and climate change is real.

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:37.400
 Society is real.

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:42.410
 Our tribal perspective is that we leave no one behind.

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:45.300
 We need to start taking care of each other.

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:48.390
 People who don't have good food, we need to start addressing that issue.

00:43:48.390 --> 00:43:56.370
 Because what happens is that, hey, society is paying for that regardless if we think we're paying for it or not, you know?

00:43:56.370 --> 00:44:06.040
 And I think that when I'm laid down to rest, this traditional knowledge is going to be in full bloom.

00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:11.850
 And at some point in time, those seeds are going to drop.

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.650
 And when the seed drops, there's no stopping it.

00:44:15.650 --> 00:44:29.780
 So all I'm doing, I've been putting here on this earth like a speck of sand on a beach, physically and in time sense.

00:44:29.780 --> 00:44:32.780
 What I'm doing right now is to be able to make my mother proud.

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:38.500
 The people who walked before me, proud.

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:56.980
 So when I go up, when I go home, the ceremonial circle is open for me to participate at a level, something I've been striving for my whole life.

00:44:56.980 --> 00:45:11.210
 With that, the seeds will fall and I'll be up there watching down on the next generation to see if I fulfill my inherent responsibility.

00:45:11.210 --> 00:45:19.160
 And my inherent responsibility is your inherent responsibility as well.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:27.180
 The more we can collaborate and communicate, understand, critically, non-critically, is important to the world.

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:31.540
 Thank you.

